WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:02.775
(brooding music)

00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:05.250
(ambient music)

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.250
- 2022 has been a really
fascinating year for movies.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.440
I'm pretty hopeful
about cinema in general.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.980
I think there's a lot of quality
stuff being made out there.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.680
It's just having to compete
with this endless void

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.120
of near infinite content

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.610
that's coming at us from
all directions right now.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.620
My top 10 list is very loosely ranked.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.290
What makes it onto the
list usually gets there

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.070
because of one or more of three things.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.310
How well the movie resonated
with me emotionally,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.550
which is very personally
subjective, obviously.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.390
My interest level in the
ideas, themes, and concepts

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.830
that it's exploring.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:44.340
And then third, how well the movie

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.670
is kind of wielding filmmaking as a form

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.240
and/or pushing the
boundaries of that medium

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.410
to do something that feels
new, original, and fresh to me.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.610
Actually trying to establish

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.710
what the best movies from a year were

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.090
is a total fool's errand,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.580
especially before the year is even over.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.250
There are still a bunch of movies

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.740
that potentially would've
made it onto this list

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.960
that I haven't had the time to see

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.704
or just aren't even
available for me to watch.

00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:16.530
But what these lists are good
for is starting a discussion.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.039
So let's hang out, talk about movies,

00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:22.830
and share with me your
top 10 from this year

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.060
in the comments below.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.700
Final note, I will keep
this list spoiler-free

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.680
so you can watch this
without worrying about

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.160
whether or not you've seen any
of the movies on this list.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.260
This video is sponsored by MUBI.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.000
Get your extended 30-day free trial

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.730
at mubi.com/thomasflight.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:42.000
First, let's get to
some honorable mentions.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.150
So there are two movies
that I got to see at Cannes

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.440
earlier this year.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.420
Lukas Dhont's "Close"

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.510
and also Kelly Reichardt's "Showing Up,"

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.240
but I'm bumping them to
the running for next year

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.200
because they're getting a wide
release in the US in 2023,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.940
but they would almost certainly be

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.440
in my top 10 for this year.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.780
Other honorable mentions are "After Yang"

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.117
from video
essayist-turned-director Kogonada.

00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:12.117
"After Yang" is a soft, kind
of gentle science fiction film

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:17.159
that I think really shows what
can be done with the genre

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.770
at that smaller scale.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:24.360
In a genre where a lot of stuff
is huge, big, high concept,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.810
often dark or sort of dystopian,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.330
it's cool to watch something
that just puts forward

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.960
a more simple, beautiful
science fiction world.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.940
I also wanna mention "The
Worst Person in the World"

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.640
from Joachim Trier that
released way back in February,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.820
which is a genuinely funny
and emotional indictment

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.740
of kind of millennial malaise

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.740
that I think still manages
to be compassionate

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:47.752
and insightful,

00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:51.660
all while doing so with
kind of stylistic flair.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.550
I also wanna mention "White Noise,"

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.840
the Noah Baumbach adaptation
of the "Don DeLillo" novel.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.290
I love the novel and I
don't know if this movie

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.150
quite manages to elevate the novel,

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.997
but it was fun to see a
really faithful adaptation

00:03:06.997 --> 00:03:09.780
in theaters and it's a fun movie.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.460
Finally, I wanna mention "Memoria"

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.757
by Apichatpong Weerasethakul.

00:03:12.757 --> 00:03:15.120
This is a movie that came out last year,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.770
but which is still slowly
touring theaters city by city.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.840
I really loved this kind of bizarre,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.570
meditative art house sci-fi,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.420
and I can't wait to watch it
again once it's out on video.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.253
But in the meantime,

00:03:28.253 --> 00:03:29.730
I definitely recommend keeping an eye out.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.470
And if it comes to your
city, check it out.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:36.470
Now, let's jump into my
loosely ranked top 10 of 2022.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:37.860
Different people are gonna want

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.060
different things from the movies,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.550
and one of the things
I've always really valued

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.650
for whatever reason is commitment.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.430
Commitment to the vision,
commitment to a concept,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.013
a story, a perspective.

00:03:49.013 --> 00:03:51.690
Nine out of 10 times,
I'd rather watch a movie

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.000
shoot for something really
interesting and specific

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.110
and miss slightly

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.142
rather than watch somebody absolutely nail

00:03:57.142 --> 00:04:00.000
what is a tried and true formula.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.750
The main appeal of Robert
Eggers' films for me is this.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.740
His 100% absolute commitment to the bit.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.320
And for me, "The Northman"
completely exemplifies this.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.480
In a year of really unique movies,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.300
there's truly no other
movie like "The Northman"

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.560
that came out this year.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.930
There's an attention
to lore and detail here

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.100
that I really appreciate,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.650
but I think most interestingly,

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.410
to me, it feels like
the movie really commits

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.287
to the perspective of
the myth it's telling.

00:04:29.287 --> 00:04:32.100
"The Northman" doesn't feel
like a movie about a myth.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.390
It feels like a myth itself,
like what Vikings might make

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.703
if they got their hands on cameras.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.670
Next up on the list is
"Decision to Leave,"

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.680
a razor-sharp neo-noir

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.870
from South Korean Director Park Chan-Wook.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.940
It's built around a convoluted,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.990
twisted mystery that rivals
the best noirs out there.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.560
This is one of those like
double indemnity kind of plots

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.070
about two people who
are absolutely perfectly

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.360
terrible for each other and
just watching them collide

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.630
in a disastrous mess that
you can't look away from.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.300
And you won't wanna look away
because Park is flying you

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.940
through the very heart
of this relationship

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.370
with the camera at breakneck speed.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.230
He's putting cameras in eyeballs

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.740
and phones and flying the
camera around through time

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.080
and moving characters in space.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.340
I've never seen someone intercut
the beginning of one scene

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.000
with the end of another in
the way he does in this movie.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.554
Like one out of every
three shots in this movie

00:05:32.554 --> 00:05:34.410
is absolutely stunning.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.380
It kind of astounds me
that he was able to pack

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.640
so much interesting, fresh,
original direction into one film

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.910
and it's a great compelling story,

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.100
but it's also worth checking out

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.442
just for studying what
he's doing in this film.

00:05:50.442 --> 00:05:51.981
(waves crashing)

00:05:51.981 --> 00:05:54.564
(guns banging)

00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:00.249
- Oh, good luck to you,

00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:03.693
whatever it is you're fighting about.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.400
- Next up is "The Banshees of Inisherin."

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:15.630
This movie brings back
the Gleason-Farrell duo

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:19.957
that I loved from one of Martin
McDonagh's earlier films,

00:06:19.957 --> 00:06:20.970
"In Bruges."

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.100
And it's a very different
relationship here,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.040
but the duo is no less compelling.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.430
It's a movie about conflict
between two friends,

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.560
but that conflict is really emblematic

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.835
of other larger conflicts

00:06:33.835 --> 00:06:37.230
in a way that is really
interesting and works really well.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.470
There's an examination of the value of art

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.050
versus the value of kind of friendship

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.970
and respect for other people.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.680
The conflict is also emblematic

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.050
of larger conflict in Ireland

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.960
and loss of meaning within
this specific community.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:52.793
And to that end,

00:06:52.793 --> 00:06:55.980
I think this movie has maybe
the strongest sense of place

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.770
out of any movie that I watched this year.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.030
So it's kind of a cliche to say,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.960
like the location is a character,
but it's really true here.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.060
Not just in how the location is shot

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.020
with the cinematography,

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:10.350
which I think is some of the
best photography I've ever seen

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:13.470
of Irish landscape in a movie,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:18.470
but also just in how
the pace of island life

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.220
kind of seeps into even the
delivery of the dialogue

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.710
and the editing of the film.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:30.060
You really feel like
you're inside this place

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.120
and that allows you to
understand the impact

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.080
that being there has on
all of the characters.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.390
And all the characters are amazing.

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.710
Some of the best performances

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.930
from a supporting cast this year, I think,

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:43.950
are in this movie.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.690
This movie immerses you in a world

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.750
and by the end you're gonna be
haunted by the same banshees

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:49.971
as everyone else on the island.

00:07:49.971 --> 00:07:51.846
(brooding music)

00:07:51.846 --> 00:07:53.040
(roof banging)

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.450
People who watch my channel know that,

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.430
like the bigger blockbuster stuff

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.320
isn't necessarily my usual vibe,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.350
but I think this was actually
kind of an amazing year

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.810
for larger blockbuster films.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.030
I think Ryan Coogler's, "Wakanda Forever"

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:09.510
was one of the most interesting MCU films

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.100
that I've seen in a long time.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.160
I think he was pushing what
you can do with an MCU film

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.607
in an interesting direction.

00:08:15.607 --> 00:08:18.330
"The Batman" is by far
the best superhero movie

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.610
I've seen in a long time
in terms of production.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:24.030
I made a video about its sound
design earlier this year.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.280
We also had "Top Gun: Maverick,"

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:27.930
which kind of came out of nowhere.

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:31.710
So I wanted to ask my podcast
co-host, Tom van der Linden,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.090
why he thought that movie

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:35.707
deserves to be among the best of the year.

00:08:35.707 --> 00:08:37.920
"Top Gun: Maverick" was one of
the biggest movies this year

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.490
in terms of theatrical audience response,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:42.840
and it's a movie I enjoyed,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.110
but I think struggled to connect with

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.270
in the same way that I
feel like a lot of people

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.010
who really enjoyed it did.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.230
And I know it's a movie
that you really liked, Tom,

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:55.290
so I'm just curious of your thoughts on

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.530
why you think "Top Gun"
deserves to be considered

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.510
one of the the best movies of this year.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.440
- I think that for me,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.200
I have no nostalgia towards the original.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.420
I kind of went into this one blind

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.160
and I expected it to be
this great action movie.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.350
You know, Tom Cruise has
been well known by now

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.680
for doing all this stuff
for real and practically

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.630
and there were this real fighter jets

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:21.840
and it was gonna be really
cool, and indeed it was.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.400
But what surprised me

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.670
was that it was actually very emotional

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:31.290
in the sense that there was
this underlying sense of,

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.870
I'd call it heroic optimism

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.150
in the way that the whole story felt.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:40.830
Like it had this energy wherein
people could come together,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.810
they could work together,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.810
elevate each other by pushing
each other to the limits,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:49.440
and then go beyond them and
achieve this common goal

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.550
or common purpose.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.200
And I felt like there
was just something so

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.674
universally beautiful about that

00:09:56.674 --> 00:10:00.120
even though there's the relation
with the military and war,

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:01.080
which we've discussed,

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.500
but which I had a little
bit of an easier time

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:05.850
to disconnect from,

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.910
seeing as I'm not from a
very militaristic country.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.320
But yeah, for me, it just,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.860
that energy is for me
what kind of resonated

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.080
the strongest with me
and what made this movie

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.090
so moving beyond its

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.070
initial experience or its
superficial experience

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:26.190
almost as a pure action film.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.750
- I really thought "Top Gun"

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:31.920
might be unabashedly
the most sincere movie

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.990
that we were gonna get this year,

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:38.400
but it might be rivaled by my pick

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.370
for favorite kind of big
blockbuster of the year,

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:46.370
which to my utter surprise,
I'm finding is "Avatar 2."

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.160
I really did not expect this
movie to be on this list

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.920
and it was a last minute edition.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.660
And despite kind of liking the original,

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:58.470
I had pretty lukewarm
expectations going into this one.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.940
And I wasn't sure at first,

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.890
there's a lot of rough edges here.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.080
This is far from a perfect movie,

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.030
but over the course of this movie's

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.740
three-hour and 12-minute runtime

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:09.840
or whatever ridiculous length it is,

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.090
it kind of, to be honest, won me over.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:13.530
This movie's VFX

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:16.680
are something that stand
completely on their own.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:21.360
Watching this is really more
like playing "Half-Life: Alyx"

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.880
in VR than it is like watching a movie,

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.820
which is not to say better,
but just a completely unique

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:30.000
and kind of original
experience on its own.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.840
That comparison

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.970
is probably either gonna
be a huge selling point

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:37.740
or downside depending on
your persuasion as a viewer.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.890
A lot of that just comes
down to how much you enjoy

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.710
the visceral experience
of watching this movie,

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.630
but I honestly don't think the visuals

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.640
and beauty of the experience

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.560
are just an empty candy coating.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.500
They're a critical part of the story.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:53.760
James Cameron is trying to get you

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.594
to fall in love with this world,

00:11:55.594 --> 00:11:59.070
and then he's putting that
world under direct attack

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.390
for dramatic effect.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.290
And with these movies,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.240
he's doing something really interesting,

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:07.230
which is modeling sort of
an attitude of engagement

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:08.640
with the world.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.210
In these movies, it's aliens
engaging with a fake world,

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.690
but he's modeling a
certain type of curiosity

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:18.870
and attention and care that
can be given to the world

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:23.520
that is really an important
one to not lose sight of

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.740
for ourselves and our own real world.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:27.870
I think we should be getting lost

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:29.970
in sort of the wonder and excitement

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.550
and joy of our own natural world.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.770
And so to have a big blockbuster movie

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:39.770
kind of exemplifying that for
like an hour in the middle

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.045
is sort of a special thing.

00:12:41.045 --> 00:12:45.120
I don't know of other movies
that come anywhere close

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.230
to doing that kind of thing

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:48.300
besides maybe like "The Tree of Life"

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.337
or the latest nature
documentary from Disney+.

00:12:51.337 --> 00:12:53.640
"The Way of Water" never quite escapes

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:56.940
the high camp of James Cameron,

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:00.060
but if I'm gonna talk
about commitment in movies.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.580
This movie is committed to whatever it is

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.200
that James Cameron is doing.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.170
It's 100% committed and unexpectedly,

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.330
I found myself on board.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:13.560
Our society is gripped
by spectacle right now.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:17.250
Spectacle that is transmitted via images.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.410
And the best,

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:22.170
most nuanced exploration of that this year

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.530
was probably Jordan Peele's "Nope."

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.748
I love that Jordan Peele
is doing this kind of thing

00:13:28.748 --> 00:13:32.167
in his latest sort of
horror adventure spin.

00:13:32.167 --> 00:13:34.380
"Nope" was the movie I had the most fun

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.940
picking apart this year.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:38.580
And despite multiple viewings,
I still have the feeling

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:42.570
that there's more left there
to be discovered in the future.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.230
The movie has an
incredible sense of rhyme.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.080
There's seemingly disconnected elements

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:53.340
that are connected through
sound design or imagery

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.560
or thematic resonance

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:58.920
that might feel disparate at first,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:02.580
but as you examine this
thing and re-watch it,

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.380
it really all kind of comes together

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.360
into this cohesive hole.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.400
I think Peele's past
movies have been great,

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.260
but I'm also excited to see him

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.920
stepping into a kind
of thematic exploration

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.440
that's a little bit more poetic.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.870
It has great sound, performances, writing.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.660
It's the first horror movie shot on IMAX.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.570
It pioneered a completely new form

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.640
of day for night cinematography.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:27.810
There's way more I could say here,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.300
but I'd just be repeating
what I've already said

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:31.800
in the two videos I made about "Nope."

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.423
So you can go check those
out if you're interested.

00:14:33.423 --> 00:14:36.590
(locomotive chuffing)

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.530
Why do the greatest
directors that have lived

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.470
do what they do, make the art they make?

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.720
This is a question that
I find fascinating.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:47.520
It's obviously one that
I've spent a lot of time

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.650
exploring on this channel,

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:53.040
and it's a question I love
because behind any piece of art

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.429
or entertainment that
captures the imagination

00:14:56.429 --> 00:14:59.610
and attention of a bunch of viewers

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.860
is a person or group of people

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.470
who made that thing for a reason.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.170
And those reasons don't
define the art itself,

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:11.190
but it's very interesting
still to examine those reasons

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:12.870
not only to better understand the art,

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:15.150
but so we can better understand ourselves,

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:16.860
why we connect with certain things,

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.810
and why we make the things we do.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:19.980
So it's kind of fascinating

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.980
anytime a director turns
the lens more inward

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:27.060
and examines their own personal journey,

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:30.060
and that can be fraught, it
doesn't always turn out well.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:34.020
Sometimes it's a more egoic
endeavor than anything,

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:36.690
but when Steven Spielberg does it,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:38.610
it's a delight because he does it

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:41.875
in only the sort of personal,

00:15:41.875 --> 00:15:45.060
completely heartfelt and captivating way

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.070
that Steven Spielberg could.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.890
Sammy Fabelman is using the camera

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:54.000
to kind of cope and process
painful situations in his life.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.680
And in a very explicit way,

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.670
we can also see Spielberg kind
of doing that with this film.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.040
The strange bittersweet thing

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.400
about telling your own story like this

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:09.720
is that it can feel like this cohesive

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:11.970
thing to the audience.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:15.510
But to the artist, these
things don't go away

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.250
when the credits roll.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:18.720
It's their real life.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:22.920
And putting your life into a
film to try to contain that

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.510
or frame it in a certain way

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:27.840
doesn't change the sort of
reality of those memories

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:29.160
or that situation.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.050
I think the beauty of "The Fabelmans"

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:35.100
is that it doesn't
sidestep Spielberg's place

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.344
as this great filmmaker
in American history,

00:16:38.344 --> 00:16:41.490
but it's still a surprisingly humble

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.430
and personal examination of the beauty

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:47.310
and limitations of the artistic process.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:51.543
- I command you, enjoy the moment.

00:16:54.769 --> 00:16:57.123
- No.
- Please.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:02.903
- No

00:17:02.903 --> 00:17:04.620
- The next best movie of the year

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:07.683
is "Mr. Morale and the
Big Steppers" by Kendrick.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.240
One of the most interesting
songs of the year to me

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:14.430
was Kendrick Lamar's "We Cried Together."

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:16.140
It's a song that takes the form

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.510
of a vicious fight between a couple.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:19.860
And there's a lot that
could be said about it,

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:22.440
but what I wanna
highlight here is, for me,

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.290
how the song captured a feeling

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:28.800
and that feeling is just
kind of what it feels like

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.130
to be listening to society right now.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:33.510
This might sound completely bizarre,

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.370
but "Triangle of Sadness"

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:38.580
does that same maneuver in comedy form.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.850
- Can you just stand here
and they'll be coming here?

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.020
- Okay.
- We'll see you.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.730
- To me, what's great
about "Triangle of Sadness"

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:48.060
is not that it has this
profound specific message

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:51.690
or incredibly original
satire or commentary.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.330
Mostly what I enjoyed about it

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:57.600
was the way that it
just managed to capture

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:01.800
the feeling of being in society right now.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.920
It taps into a vibe in a comedic way

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:08.670
and that vibe is the absurdity

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:09.960
of being in the society

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:11.435
where things are kind of out of control

00:18:11.435 --> 00:18:14.580
and a storm is battering the ship

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.380
and the upper class and
the lower class alike

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:18.930
are all just kind of having
to go about their business

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:21.300
and pretend like everything's fine

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:24.540
while the captain of the ship is drunk

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:29.013
and arguing about economic
theory with a Russian capitalist.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:31.530
I love impressionist art.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.300
There's few things as beautiful to me

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:37.650
as these paintings where you
can see the craft itself,

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:41.160
the impression of each brush stroke.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:42.960
And if you really zoom in,

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:46.170
you'll get lost in a world of abstraction

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:49.050
and won't be able to make
out any of the details.

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.990
But as you pull back slowly,

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.900
the image itself kind of snaps into view.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:59.010
I can think of no better way
to really describe "Aftersun,"

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:02.220
a stunning debut from
Director Charlotte Wells.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:04.200
The movie doesn't announce
to you what it's doing.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:06.270
Instead, you just sit there

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:09.690
with a series of small
light brush strokes.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.420
A look here, a moment of dialogue there.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:15.120
You can tell what's going on
literally with the characters,

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.090
but the why behind what's happening

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.760
is faint and obscured at first.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.460
All you can see are
these individual shapes.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.170
But as you slip deeper into the film,

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.780
it feels not like the movie
is telling you its story,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:29.850
but as if it is emerging

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:32.520
from those strokes as a complete image.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:36.390
You have a sense watching
it of discovering the story,

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.480
and then you realize
that all those shapes,

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:42.540
all those individual strokes are memories,

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:45.720
an effect that places
you in the perspective

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.500
of an adult character
actually looking back on

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:52.110
and remembering these events.

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:56.160
And for them, the story is also emerging

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:58.590
out of a collection of abstract strokes.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.200
It's only from their
perspective as an adult now

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:04.350
that the story is snapping into place.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:09.060
It's a beautiful shattering
film that is gentle, heartfelt,

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:12.750
nuanced, and it displays
how emotion can be complex

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:16.530
and confused more clearly than I think

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:18.603
any other film I've seen recently has.

00:20:20.287 --> 00:20:22.920
"Tar" was the film I was
probably least expecting

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.300
to see this year.

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:25.830
I knew nothing about it going in.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:28.230
I just knew this is a movie.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.380
Cate Blanchett plays a composer.

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:33.000
What unfolded surprised me

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:36.150
by being one of the most dense,
fascinating character pieces

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:38.970
that I've seen in the theaters in years.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.580
At the start of the film,
you're thrown into the deep end

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:43.560
of the world of pretentious,

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.800
ostentatious classical composers.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.710
And the scenes that follow
carefully sketch out a character

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:53.730
that is haunted by their
own misuse of power.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.250
We've seen this kind
of character in movies

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.930
endlessly time and time again.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:00.660
It's one of film's most
time honored tradition

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:05.660
to examine the minutiae of
terrible, powerful people.

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.220
But Director Todd Field is
flipping things on their head

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:11.550
just enough that allows us a fresh glimpse

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:15.420
into this dynamic and how it can play out

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:16.890
not just across gender,

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:20.790
but across all kinds of
institutions of power

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:24.150
or places where we venerate a person

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:28.110
or artist above the value
of what they're doing

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:29.250
in and of itself

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:32.610
Lydia Tar would have you think
that it's only about the art,

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.790
but what haunts her is
that, really, for her,

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.440
it's about anything but that.

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.900
I think this movie is getting
misrepresented a little bit

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.150
as being a movie about cancel culture,

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:44.190
and it touches on that as a theme,

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:46.650
but I think it goes much, much deeper.

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:49.110
Above all, it's a rich,

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.510
expertly crafted character study

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:53.850
centered around maybe the
year's best performance

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:55.260
from Cate Blanchett.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.300
This is a movie that I
know has more to offer

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.010
than what I absorbed
the first time through

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.743
and I can't wait to explore it further.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:04.523
Back in April,

00:22:04.523 --> 00:22:06.720
before "Everything Everywhere
All at Once" hit theaters,

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:09.630
A24 and the writers and
directors of the movie,

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.190
the Daniels, released a letter

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:12.540
explaining some of the motivation

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.550
behind why they made the movie.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.310
In it, they talked about the
weird feeling and pressure

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:20.700
of asking an audience for
two hours of their time

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.610
in a world where it kind of
feels like there's already

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:27.720
objectively too much
stuff to ever be consumed.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:28.920
I think it's a valid question.

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.720
It's one I think about a lot.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.200
Why should you add to the noise

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:38.670
when it feels like what people
really need is less of it?

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.657
In the letter, they say this.

00:22:40.657 --> 00:22:42.780
"We realized if we were
going to make a film

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.840
and ask an audience to
give us that precious time,

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:49.020
the only responsible thing to do in return

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:52.590
was to blow their minds and
change their lives forever.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:56.520
Or at the very least, we
were going to attempt that."

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:57.937
At the time I read the letter, I was like,

00:22:57.937 --> 00:22:59.730
"Okay, guys, chill."

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:03.330
What I didn't expect
was a movie that would

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:06.090
actually maybe come close to doing that.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.330
I don't know if I would
say "Everything Everywhere"

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:10.950
changed my life,

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:15.950
but it did manage to tap
into the nerve of something

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:18.630
and portray certain ideas

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:21.960
that I would say are currently
kind of changing my life.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:24.938
This is the only film that
has come anywhere close

00:23:24.938 --> 00:23:27.210
to capturing the sheer chaos

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:30.390
of what life has sometimes felt like

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:31.770
over the past few years.

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:33.090
A feeling I personally linked

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.460
to spending too much time on
the internet and social media

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.580
in a video that I made about
the movie earlier this summer

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:39.600
that you can check out.

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:43.170
But this movie also captured
a crucial piece of something

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.210
that was very important to me this year.

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:49.110
There's a moment amid all the
absurd chaos of this movie

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:50.850
that I think is very powerful.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:53.910
And it's powerful only
because of how chaotic

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:56.310
and completely over the top and ridiculous

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:57.720
the rest of the movie is.

00:23:57.720 --> 00:23:59.430
I think it's very significant

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:02.220
that before there's any real change

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.382
for the characters in this film,

00:24:04.382 --> 00:24:06.630
there's this moment where they completely

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:11.460
stepped back into utter
stillness and quiet

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:18.390
and examined the value of
the mystery of existence

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:19.833
at its very core.

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.650
The biggest lesson I
learned in my life this year

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:28.770
and one that has significantly
impacted my mental health

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:33.210
for the better is that the
answer to the overwhelming chaos

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:35.880
can't be found within the noise,

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:40.500
but only by removing yourself
from it into stillness.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:42.300
It's why this sudden cut

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:44.820
to just two rocks sitting in the desert

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:47.340
had the most emotional impact on me

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:50.460
out of anything I saw
in a movie this year.

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:52.680
It's only by taking
time away from the chaos

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.750
that you can really recenter yourself

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:57.210
and humbly return with an understanding

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:59.370
of what truly matters.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:00.210
It's the holidays

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:02.700
and I tend to think of
this as movie season,

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:04.560
not just because a bunch of great stuff

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:06.570
tends to drop at the end of the year,

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:08.010
but also because when you get together

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:08.970
with friends or family,

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:11.580
it's a great time to watch some movies.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.770
That makes it the
perfect time to check out

